    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p69-q19-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Express the height of the rectangle in terms of $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\left(6-\\frac35x\\right)\\text{ cm}$.",
          "worked": "Let the rectangle's height be $h$ cm. Take $B$ as the origin, measuring horizontal distance positively towards $A$ and height upwards. Then $A=(10,0)$, $C=(0,6)$ and the rectangle's upper-left vertex is $(x,h)$.\n\nThe gradient of $AC$ is\n$$\\begin{align*}m&=\\frac{0-6}{10-0}\\\\m&=-\\frac35.\\end{align*}$$\nUsing point-gradient form through $C$,\n$$\\begin{align*}h-6&=-\\frac35(x-0)\\\\h&=6-\\frac35x.\\end{align*}$$\nThe height is $\\left(6-\\frac35x\\right)\\text{ cm}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 56
      },
      {
        "id": "p69-q19-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that the area $A(x)$ of the rectangle is given by a quadratic expression.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$A(x)=6x-\\frac35x^2\\text{ cm}^2$.\n\nMultiply $x$ by the height $6-\\frac35x$.",
          "worked": "Area is width multiplied by height.\n$$\\begin{align*}A(x)&=x\\left(6-\\frac35x\\right)\\\\A(x)&=6x-\\frac35x^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe area is measured in $\\text{cm}^2$. This is a quadratic expression because its $x^2$ coefficient is nonzero.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 22
      },
      {
        "id": "p69-q19-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Find the value of $x$ that maximises the area of the rectangle.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=5\\text{ cm}$.\n\nThe concave-down area parabola has its maximum halfway between its zeros, $0$ and $10$.",
          "worked": "$$A(x)=\\frac35x(10-x).$$\nThe zeros are $0$ and $10$. The coefficient of $x^2$ is negative, so the parabola is concave down and its vertex gives a maximum. By symmetry,\n$$\\begin{align*}x_{\\max}&=\\frac{0+10}{2}\\\\x_{\\max}&=5.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $0<5<10$, this lies within the geometric domain. The area is maximised when $x=5\\text{ cm}$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 46
      },
      {
        "id": "p69-q19-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "What is the maximum possible area?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$15\\text{ cm}^2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}A(5)&=6(5)-\\frac35(5)^2\\\\A(5)&=15\\text{ cm}^2.\\end{align*}$$\nThe maximum possible area is $15\\text{ cm}^2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ],
